{"article_id": "laysumm_elife_0006", "source_dataset": "eLife", "original_index": 6, "mode": "factuality_chase", "model_key": "gemini25_flash_non_thinking", "evidence_rows": [{"article_id": "laysumm_elife_0006", "source_dataset": "eLife", "original_index": 6, "mode": "factuality_chase", "evidence_row_id": "row_0001", "abstract_sentence_idx": 1, "abstract_claim": "Many decisions are thought to arise via the accumulation of noisy evidence to a threshold or bound.", "core_keep_af": "Many decisions are thought to arise from the accumulation of noisy evidence until a certain threshold is reached.", "expert_summary_hint": "One theory of decision-making is that the brain simultaneously accumulates evidence for each of the options it is considering, until one option exceeds a threshold and is declared the 'winner'. This theory is known as bounded evidence accumulation.", "lay_context": "This model suggests that the brain gathers information over time until enough evidence for one choice crosses a certain point, leading to a decision.", "story_role": "background", "support_af_ids": ["laysumm_elife_0006_cand_0001", "laysumm_elife_0006_cand_0012"], "allowed_evidence_spans": ["Many decisions are thought to arise via the accumulation of noisy evidence to a threshold or bound .", "The decision terminates when the accumulated evidence for one alternative , termed a decision variable ( DV ) , reaches a threshold or bound , thereby determining both the choice and the decision time ."], "must_use_terms": ["decisions", "accumulation of noisy evidence", "threshold", "bound"], "forbidden_overgeneralizations": ["all decisions", "always"], "high_risk_check_facts": []}, {"article_id": "laysumm_elife_0006", "source_dataset": "eLife", "original_index": 6, "mode": "factuality_chase", "evidence_row_id": "row_0002", "abstract_sentence_idx": 4, "abstract_claim": "An increase in noise should lead to faster decisions, reduced accuracy and, paradoxically, higher confidence.", "core_keep_af": "The bounded evidence accumulation theory predicts that an increase in noise should lead to faster decisions, reduced accuracy, and paradoxically, higher confidence.", "expert_summary_hint": "It predicts that increasing the noisiness of the available information decreases the accuracy of decisions made in response. Counterintuitively, it also predicts that such an increase in noise speeds up decision-making and increases confidence levels.", "lay_context": "This prediction is counterintuitive because one might expect more noise to slow down decisions and decrease confidence.", "story_role": "finding", "support_af_ids": ["laysumm_elife_0006_cand_0003", "laysumm_elife_0006_cand_0073", "laysumm_elife_0006_cand_0076"], "allowed_evidence_spans": ["An increase in noise should lead to faster decisions , reduced accuracy and , paradoxically , higher confidence .", "The important point is that by increasing noise , the volatility manipulation accelerates the dispersion of the decision variable away from its expected value and nearer the termination bounds , hence faster RT .", "Increased volatility should affect confidence because it mimics an increase in the diffusion rate ."], "must_use_terms": ["increase in noise", "faster decisions", "reduced accuracy", "higher confidence", "paradoxically"], "forbidden_overgeneralizations": ["noise always causes these effects"], "high_risk_check_facts": [{"fact": "An increase in noise is predicted to lead to faster decisions, reduced accuracy, and higher confidence.", "slot_type": "direction", "source_span": "An increase in noise should lead to faster decisions , reduced accuracy and , paradoxically , higher confidence ."}]}, {"article_id": "laysumm_elife_0006", "source_dataset": "eLife", "original_index": 6, "mode": "factuality_chase", "evidence_row_id": "row_0003", "abstract_sentence_idx": 5, "abstract_claim": "To test these predictions, we introduce a novel sensory manipulation that mimics the addition of unbiased noise to motion-selective regions of visual cortex, which we verified with neuronal recordings from macaque areas MT/MST.", "core_keep_af": "The researchers developed a novel sensory manipulation to mimic unbiased noise in motion-selective visual cortex regions, which was verified by neuronal recordings in macaque areas MT/MST.", "expert_summary_hint": "Using a newly developed method, the noisiness of the dot motion could be changed between trials. The effectiveness of this technique was confirmed by recording the activity of neurons in the region of the monkey brain that processes visual motion information.", "lay_context": "This manipulation allowed them to specifically control the level of 'noise' in the visual information processed by the brain, without biasing the actual motion direction.", "story_role": "method", "support_af_ids": ["laysumm_elife_0006_cand_0004", "laysumm_elife_0006_cand_0005", "laysumm_elife_0006_cand_0025", "laysumm_elife_0006_cand_0027"], "allowed_evidence_spans": ["To test these predictions , we introduce a novel sensory manipulation that mimics the addition of unbiased noise to motion-selective regions of visual cortex, which we verified with neuronal recordings from macaque areas MT/MST .", "To test these predictions , we introduce a novel sensory manipulation that mimics the addition of unbiased noise to motion-selective regions of visual cortex , which we verified with neuronal recordings from macaque areas MT/MST .", "We achieved this with a manipulation of the motion information in a random dot motion ( RDM ) display , by adding a second level of randomness which increased its volatility but was unbiased with respect to the strength and direction of motion evidence .", "We verified that the manipulation has the desired properties by recording from direction selective neurons in the middle temporal ( MT ) and medial superior temporal ( MST ) areas of the macaque visual cortex ."], "must_use_terms": ["novel sensory manipulation", "unbiased noise", "motion-selective regions of visual cortex", "neuronal recordings", "macaque areas MT/MST"], "forbidden_overgeneralizations": ["any noise", "all brain regions"], "high_risk_check_facts": [{"fact": "The novel sensory manipulation was verified using neuronal recordings from macaque areas MT/MST.", "slot_type": "method", "source_span": "To test these predictions , we introduce a novel sensory manipulation that mimics the addition of unbiased noise to motion-selective regions of visual cortex , which we verified with neuronal recordings from macaque areas MT/MST ."}, {"fact": "The researchers verified the desired properties of their manipulation by recording from direction-selective neurons in the middle temporal (MT) and medial superior temporal (MST) areas of the macaque visual cortex.", "slot_type": "organism", "source_span": "We verified that the manipulation has the desired properties by recording from direction selective neurons in the middle temporal ( MT ) and medial superior temporal ( MST ) areas of the macaque visual cortex ."}]}, {"article_id": "laysumm_elife_0006", "source_dataset": "eLife", "original_index": 6, "mode": "factuality_chase", "evidence_row_id": "row_0004", "abstract_sentence_idx": 6, "abstract_claim": "For both humans and monkeys, increasing the noise induced faster decisions and greater confidence over a range of stimuli for which accuracy was minimally impaired.", "core_keep_af": "In both humans and monkeys, increased noise led to faster decisions and greater confidence, with only minimal impairment to accuracy.", "expert_summary_hint": "In both humans and monkeys, increasing the noisiness associated with the movement of the dots led to faster and more confident decision-making, just as the bounded evidence accumulation framework predicts.", "lay_context": "This finding shows that even when the information was noisier, participants made decisions more quickly and felt more certain about them, without a significant drop in how often they were correct.", "story_role": "finding", "support_af_ids": ["laysumm_elife_0006_cand_0006", "laysumm_elife_0006_cand_0066", "laysumm_elife_0006_cand_0068"], "allowed_evidence_spans": ["For both humans and monkeys , increasing the noise induced faster decisions and greater confidence over a range of stimuli for which accuracy was minimally impaired .", "The main effect of high volatility was to decrease RTs , particularly at the weakest motion strengths ( Figure 3B , bottom row , red ) .", "The manipulation affected the accuracy only subtly , and this was not statistically reliable for individual subjects in the RT task ( Figure 3B , top row; for the four subjects: p=[0 . 35 , 0 . 65 , 0 . 2 , 0 . 26] , Equation 17 , likelihood-ratio test ) ."], "must_use_terms": ["humans", "monkeys", "increasing the noise", "faster decisions", "greater confidence", "accuracy was minimally impaired"], "forbidden_overgeneralizations": ["accuracy was unaffected", "always faster", "always more confident"], "high_risk_check_facts": [{"fact": "For both humans and monkeys, increasing the noise resulted in faster decisions and greater confidence across a range of stimuli where accuracy was minimally affected.", "slot_type": "direction", "source_span": "For both humans and monkeys , increasing the noise induced faster decisions and greater confidence over a range of stimuli for which accuracy was minimally impaired ."}, {"fact": "The volatility manipulation affected accuracy only subtly and was not statistically reliable for individual subjects in the reaction time task.", "slot_type": "measurement", "source_span": "The manipulation affected the accuracy only subtly , and this was not statistically reliable for individual subjects in the RT task ( Figure 3B , top row; for the four subjects: p=[0 . 35 , 0 . 65 , 0 . 2 , 0 . 26] , Equation 17 , likelihood-ratio test ) ."}]}, {"article_id": "laysumm_elife_0006", "source_dataset": "eLife", "original_index": 6, "mode": "factuality_chase", "evidence_row_id": "row_0005", "abstract_sentence_idx": 7, "abstract_claim": "The magnitude of the effects was in agreement with predictions of a bounded evidence accumulation model.", "core_keep_af": "The observed effects of increased noise on decision speed and confidence were consistent with the predictions of a bounded evidence accumulation model.", "expert_summary_hint": "In both humans and monkeys, increasing the noisiness associated with the movement of the dots led to faster and more confident decision-making, just as the bounded evidence accumulation framework predicts.", "lay_context": "This suggests that the bounded evidence accumulation model accurately describes how the brain processes noisy information to make decisions.", "story_role": "implication", "support_af_ids": ["laysumm_elife_0006_cand_0007", "laysumm_elife_0006_cand_0100", "laysumm_elife_0006_cand_0132"], "allowed_evidence_spans": ["The magnitude of the effects was in agreement with predictions of a bounded evidence accumulation model .", "The overall quality of the fits—across all tasks and both species—indicates that the influence of motion strength and volatility on choice , reaction time and confidence can be explained by a common mechanism of bounded evidence accumulation .", "and ( iii ) showed how a framework based on the bounded accumulation of evidence can account for the joint effect of volatility on choice , reaction time and confidence ."], "must_use_terms": ["agreement", "predictions", "bounded evidence accumulation model"], "forbidden_overgeneralizations": ["proved the model", "perfect agreement"], "high_risk_check_facts": []}], "evidence_row_count": 5, "parse_error": false, "validation_errors": [], "raw_response": null}
