y_hint": "Quality control processes have been well-studied in some parts of the cell but not others.", "lay_context": "While general protein quality control systems exist, the specific pathways for misfolded proteins within mitochondria are not yet well understood.", "story_role": "motivation", "support_af_ids": ["laysumm_elife_0070_cand_0002", "laysumm_elife_0070_cand_0017"], "allowed_evidence_spans": ["However , quality control ( QC ) pathways for misfolded mitochondrial proteins remain poorly defined .", "In contrast to mitophagy , mitochondria-associated degradation ( MAD ) pathways for individual misfolded or damaged mitochondrial proteins are not as well-established in mammals or yeast ."], "must_use_terms": ["quality control pathways", "misfolded mitochondrial proteins", "poorly defined"], "forbidden_overgeneralizations": ["no quality control pathways", "completely unknown"], "high_risk_check_facts": []}, {"article_id": "laysumm_elife_0070", "source_dataset": "eLife", "original_index": 70, "mode": "factuality_chase", "evidence_row_id": "row_0003", "abstract_sentence_idx": 3, "abstract_claim": "This study established temperature-sensitive (ts-) peripheral mitochondrial outer membrane (MOM) proteins as novel model QC substrates in Saccharomyces cerevisiae.", "core_keep_af": "The study established temperature-sensitive (ts-) peripheral mitochondrial outer membrane (MOM) proteins as new model quality control substrates in Saccharomyces cerevisiae (yeast).", "expert_summary_hint": "To do this, they used baker’s yeast, a model laboratory organism that shares many fundamental properties with animal cells, but which is easier to manipulate genetically. The quality control process was studied using two mitochondrial proteins that had been mutated to make them sensitive to changes in temperature.", "lay_context": "To investigate these pathways, researchers used baker's yeast and created temperature-sensitive proteins on the mitochondrial outer membrane that would misfold when the temperature increased, acting as model substrates for quality control.", "story_role": "method", "support_af_ids": ["laysumm_elife_0070_cand_0003", "laysumm_elife_0070_cand_0085", "laysumm_elife_0070_cand_0023"], "allowed_evidence_spans": ["Here , we establish temperature-sensitive ( ts- ) peripheral mitochondrial outer membrane ( MOM ) proteins as novel model QC substrates in Saccharomyces cerevisiae .", "While previous mitochondrial UPS substrates have consisted primarily of native proteins ( Belgareh-Touzé et al . , 2017; Cohen et al . , 2008; Heo et al . , 2010; Wu et al . , 2016 ) , sam35-2HAts and sen2-1HAts contain mutations that render them unstable at the non-permissive temperature , and thus mimic damaged , misfolded proteins.", "The ts- nature of these substrates, coupled with their tight association with the MOM, enabled study of their mitochondrial degradation without concerns of mislocalization to the cytosol or elsewhere."], "must_use_terms": ["temperature-sensitive", "mitochondrial outer membrane", "model QC substrates", "Saccharomyces cerevisiae", "yeast"], "forbidden_overgeneralizations": ["all mitochondrial proteins", "all organisms"], "high_risk_check_facts": [{"fact": "The study used baker’s yeast (Saccharomyces cerevisiae) as a model organism.", "slot_type": "organism", "source_span": "Here , we establish temperature-sensitive ( ts- ) peripheral mitochondrial outer membrane ( MOM ) proteins as novel model QC substrates in Saccharomyces cerevisiae ."}, {"fact": "The model substrates were temperature-sensitive (ts-) peripheral mitochondrial outer membrane (MOM) proteins.", "slot_type": "entity", "source_span": "Here , we establish temperature-sensitive ( ts- ) peripheral mitochondrial outer membrane ( MOM ) proteins as novel model QC substrates in Saccharomyces cerevisiae ."}]}, {"article_id": "laysumm_elife_0070", "source_dataset": "eLife", "original_index": 70, "mode": "factuality_chase", "evidence_row_id": "row_0004", "abstract_sentence_idx": 4, "abstract_claim": "The ts- proteins sen2-1HAts and sam35-2HAts are degraded from the MOM by the ubiquitin-proteasome system.", "core_keep_af": "The temperature-sensitive proteins sen2-1HAts and sam35-2HAts are degraded from the mitochondrial outer membrane by the ubiquitin-proteasome system.", "expert_summary_hint": "When several ubiquitin tags are attached to a protein, forming chains of ubiquitin, it is transported to a large molecular machine within the cell called the proteasome. The proteasome unravels the protein and breaks it down into its constituent building blocks, which can then be used to create new proteins.", "lay_context": "These misfolded proteins, sen2-1HAts and sam35-2HAts, are removed from the mitochondrial outer membrane through a process called the ubiquitin-proteasome system, where ubiquitin tags mark them for degradation by the proteasome.", "story_role": "finding", "support_af_ids": ["laysumm_elife_0070_cand_0004", "laysumm_elife_0070_cand_0047", "laysumm_elife_0070_cand_0079"], "allowed_evidence_spans": ["The ts- proteins sen2-1HAts and sam35-2HAts are degraded from the MOM by the ubiquitin-proteasome system .", "Taken together , these data establish roles for ubiquitination and proteasomal degradation in the turnover of sam35-2HAts and sen2-1HAts at the MOM .", "However, fractionation data in the proteasome mutant strain (Figure 2C and Figure 2—figure supplement 1E) strongly suggests that ts- protein degradation occurs from the mitochondria."], "must_use_terms": ["sen2-1HAts", "sam35-2HAts", "degraded", "mitochondrial outer membrane", "ubiquitin-proteasome system"], "forbidden_overgeneralizations": ["all mitochondrial proteins", "only this system"], "high_risk_check_facts": [{"fact": "The degradation occurs from the mitochondrial outer membrane (MOM).", "slot_type": "entity", "source_span": "The ts- proteins sen2-1HAts and sam35-2HAts are degraded from the MOM by the ubiquitin-proteasome system ."}]}, {"article_id": "laysumm_elife_0070", "source_dataset": "eLife", "original_index": 70, "mode": "factuality_chase", "evidence_row_id": "row_0005", "abstract_sentence_idx": 5, "abstract_claim": "Ubiquitination of sen2-1HAts is mediated by the ubiquitin ligase (E3) Ubr1, while sam35-2HAts is ubiquitinated primarily by San1.", "core_keep_af": "Ubiquitination of sen2-1HAts is mediated by the ubiquitin ligase (E3) Ubr1, while sam35-2HAts is ubiquitinated primarily by San1.", "expert_summary_hint": "Then another class of proteins attaches a molecular tag, known as ubiquitin, to the misshapen proteins.", "lay_context": "Specifically, the ubiquitin tag is attached to sen2-1HAts by the ubiquitin ligase Ubr1, and to sam35-2HAts primarily by San1.", "story_role": "mechanism", "support_af_ids": ["laysumm_elife_0070_cand_0005", "laysumm_elife_0070_cand_0006", "laysumm_elife_0070_cand_0096"], "allowed_evidence_spans": ["Ubiquitination of sen2-1HAts is mediated by the ubiquitin ligase ( E3 ) Ubr1 , while sam35-2HAts is ubiquitinated primarily by San1 .", "Ubiquitination of sen2-1HAts is mediated by the ubiquitin ligase ( E3 ) Ubr1 , while sam35-2HAts is ubiquitinated primarily by San1 .", "Instead, San1 and Ubr1, which have broad roles as quality control E3s for misfolded cytosolic and nuclear proteins ( Amm et al . , 2016; Amm and Wolf , 2016; Eisele and Wolf , 2008; Gardner et al . , 2005; Guerriero et al . , 2013; Heck et al . , 2010; Khosrow-Khavar et al . , 2012; Lewis and Pelham , 2009; Nillegoda et al . , 2010; Prasad et al . , 2012; Prasad et al . , 2018; Samant et al . , 2018; Summers et al . , 2013 ) , are required to degrade mitochondrial ts- substrates."], "must_use_terms": ["ubiquitination", "sen2-1HAts", "Ubr1", "sam35-2HAts", "San1", "ubiquitin ligase"], "forbidden_overgeneralizations": ["all ubiquitination", "only these ligases"], "high_risk_check_facts": [{"fact": "Ubr1 mediates ubiquitination of sen2-1HAts.", "slot_type": "causality", "source_span": "Ubiquitination of sen2-1HAts is mediated by the ubiquitin ligase ( E3 ) Ubr1 , while sam35-2HAts is ubiquitinated primarily by San1 ."}, {"fact": "San1 primarily mediates ubiquitination of sam35-2HAts.", "slot_type": "causality", "source_span": "Ubiquitination of sen2-1HAts is mediated by the ubiquitin ligase ( E3 ) Ubr1 , while sam35-2HAts is ubiquitinated primarily by San1 ."}]}, {"article_id": "laysumm_elife_0070", "source_dataset": "eLife", "original_index": 70, "mode": "factuality_chase", "evidence_row_id": "row_0006", "abstract_sentence_idx": 6, "abstract_claim": "Mitochondria-associated degradation (MAD) of both substrates requires the SSA family of Hsp70s and the Hsp40 Sis1, providing the first evidence for chaperone involvement in MAD.", "core_keep_af": "Mitochondria-associated degradation (MAD) of both sen2-1HAts and sam35-2HAts requires the SSA family of Hsp70s and the Hsp40 Sis1.", "expert_summary_hint": "First, sets of proteins known as chaperones recognize the misfolded proteins.", "lay_context": "This study found that the degradation process, called mitochondria-associated degradation (MAD), requires specific chaperone proteins: the SSA family of Hsp70s and the Hsp40 Sis1. This is the first evidence of chaperones being involved in MAD.", "story_role": "finding", "support_af_ids": ["laysumm_elife_0070_cand_0007", "laysumm_elife_0070_cand_0008", "laysumm_elife_0070_cand_0060"], "allowed_evidence_spans": ["Mitochondria-associated degradation ( MAD ) of both substrates requires the SSA family of Hsp70s and the Hsp40 Sis1 , providing the first evidence for chaperone involvement in MAD .", "Mitochondria-associated degradation ( MAD ) of both substrates requires the SSA family of Hsp70s and the Hsp40 Sis1 , providing the first evidence for chaperone involvement in MAD .", "These results establish that the degradation of the mitochondrial QC substrates is dependent on both Hsp70 and Hsp40 chaperones , but independent of nuclear import ."], "must_use_terms": ["Mitochondria-associated degradation",