sly .", "We trained participants on only a subset of shape/visuomotor-mapping pairs and after training we tested for generalisation to other target shapes along the shape scale .", "Afterwards, using catch trials for which the task-relevant visual feedback was eliminated, we tested participants’ pointing performance to target shapes that had not been used explicitly during training."], "must_use_terms": ["after training", "without feedback", "different target shapes", "not been learned previously"], "forbidden_overgeneralizations": ["all shapes", "with feedback"], "high_risk_check_facts": [{"fact": "The study specifically tested performance on target shape morphs that were either intermediate (interpolation) or outside (extrapolation) the trained shapes.", "slot_type": "method", "source_span": "In this first experiment, we specifically tested participants’ performance on different target shape morphs that could either be in-between the two training shapes along the shape morph axis (interpolation) or that could come from further outside (extrapolation)."}]}, {"article_id": "laysumm_plos_0228", "source_dataset": "PLOS", "original_index": 228, "mode": "factuality_chase", "evidence_row_id": "row_0006", "abstract_sentence_idx": 12, "abstract_claim": "The experimental results show that, although learning the associations requires explicit awareness of the cues’ role, participants apply the mapping corresponding to the trained shape that is most similar to the current one, consistent with the Bayesian learning model.", "core_keep_af": "Learning the associations required explicit awareness of the cues' role, and participants applied the visuomotor mapping of the trained shape most similar to the current one, consistent with the Bayesian learning model.", "expert_summary_hint": "We found that participants did not use the trained linear relationship , but rather weighed the learned shape-contexts according to their similarity to the current test-shape .", "lay_context": "Thi