{"article_id": "laysumm_plos_1309", "source_dataset": "PLOS", "original_index": 1309, "mode": "factuality_chase", "model_key": "gemini25_flash_non_thinking", "evidence_rows": [{"article_id": "laysumm_plos_1309", "source_dataset": "PLOS", "original_index": 1309, "mode": "factuality_chase", "evidence_row_id": "row_0001", "abstract_sentence_idx": 1, "abstract_claim": "Abundance of substrate receptor subunits of Cullin-RING ubiquitin ligases (CRLs) is tightly controlled to maintain the full repertoire of CRLs.", "core_keep_af": "The abundance of substrate receptor subunits of Cullin-RING ubiquitin ligases (CRLs) is tightly controlled to maintain the full repertoire of CRLs.", "expert_summary_hint": "Maintaining a balanced repertoire of diverse SCF complexes that represent the entire cellular panel of substrate adapters is challenging.", "lay_context": "Cullin-RING ubiquitin ligases (CRLs) are protein complexes that regulate other proteins by attaching ubiquitin to them. The 'substrate receptor subunits' are the parts of CRLs that recognize which proteins to ubiquitylate. Their levels must be carefully managed to ensure the CRLs can perform all their functions.", "story_role": "background", "support_af_ids": ["laysumm_plos_1309_cand_0001", "laysumm_plos_1309_cand_0084"], "allowed_evidence_spans": ["Abundance of substrate receptor subunits of Cullin-RING ubiquitin ligases ( CRLs ) is tightly controlled to maintain the full repertoire of CRLs .", "It is thus critical to regulate F-box protein levels ."], "must_use_terms": ["Cullin-RING ubiquitin ligases", "CRLs", "substrate receptor subunits", "tightly controlled"], "forbidden_overgeneralizations": ["all proteins", "always controlled"], "high_risk_check_facts": []}, {"article_id": "laysumm_plos_1309", "source_dataset": "PLOS", "original_index": 1309, "mode": "factuality_chase", "evidence_row_id": "row_0002", "abstract_sentence_idx": 2, "abstract_claim": "Unbalanced levels can lead to sequestration of CRL core components by a few overabundant substrate receptors.", "core_keep_af": "Unbalanced levels of substrate receptor subunits can lead to sequestration of CRL core components by a few overabundant substrate receptors.", "expert_summary_hint": "Depending on the cell type , hundreds of different F-box proteins can compete for the single binding site on the common SCF core complex .", "lay_context": "If there are too many of certain substrate receptors, they can 'sequester' or tie up the core components of CRLs, preventing other CRLs from forming and functioning correctly.", "story_role": "background", "support_af_ids": ["laysumm_plos_1309_cand_0002", "laysumm_plos_1309_cand_0014"], "allowed_evidence_spans": ["Unbalanced levels can lead to sequestration of CRL core components by a few overabundant substrate receptors .", "Accordingly , over expression of a single F-box protein in yeast can change the balanced distribution and diversity of available SCF complexes by sequestering cullin and Skp1 and thus block formation of functional SCF complexes with other F-box proteins [10 , 11 , 14] ."], "must_use_terms": ["unbalanced levels", "sequestration", "CRL core components", "overabundant substrate receptors"], "forbidden_overgeneralizations": ["always leads to disease", "only affects a few proteins"], "high_risk_check_facts": []}, {"article_id": "laysumm_plos_1309", "source_dataset": "PLOS", "original_index": 1309, "mode": "factuality_chase", "evidence_row_id": "row_0003", "abstract_sentence_idx": 3, "abstract_claim": "Numerous diseases, including cancer, have been associated with misregulation of substrate receptor components, particularly for the largest class of CRLs, the SCF ligases.", "core_keep_af": "Numerous diseases, including cancer, are associated with misregulation of substrate receptor components, particularly for SCF ligases.", "expert_summary_hint": "alterations in levels of F-box proteins has been linked to diseases including cancer .", "lay_context": "Problems with regulating these substrate receptor components, especially in a major group of CRLs called SCF ligases, have been linked to many diseases, including cancer.", "story_role": "motivation", "support_af_ids": ["laysumm_plos_1309_cand_0003", "laysumm_plos_1309_cand_0015"], "allowed_evidence_spans": ["Numerous diseases , including cancer , have been associated with misregulation of substrate receptor components , particularly for the largest class of CRLs , the SCF ligases .", "Many F-box proteins control degradation of critical oncogenes and tumor suppressors and variation in their abundance has been linked to cancer [15 , 16] ."], "must_use_terms": ["diseases", "cancer", "misregulation", "substrate receptor components", "SCF ligases"], "forbidden_overgeneralizations": ["all diseases", "only cancer"], "high_risk_check_facts": []}, {"article_id": "laysumm_plos_1309", "source_dataset": "PLOS", "original_index": 1309, "mode": "factuality_chase", "evidence_row_id": "row_0004", "abstract_sentence_idx": 5, "abstract_claim": "Here we describe an additional pathway for regulation of F-box proteins on the example of yeast Met30.", "core_keep_af": "This study describes an additional pathway for regulation of F-box proteins, using yeast Met30 as an example.", "expert_summary_hint": "Studying the yeast F-box protein Met30 as a model , we have uncovered a novel mechanism for degradation of F-box proteins .", "lay_context": "This research identifies a new way that F-box proteins, a type of substrate receptor, are regulated. The study uses the Met30 protein in yeast as a model to understand this process.", "story_role": "finding", "support_af_ids": ["laysumm_plos_1309_cand_0005", "laysumm_plos_1309_cand_0024"], "allowed_evidence_spans": ["Here we describe an additional pathway for regulation of F-box proteins on the example of yeast Met30 .", "Here we report such an additional mechanism for Met30 regulation in addition to autoubiquitylation ."], "must_use_terms": ["additional pathway", "regulation of F-box proteins", "yeast Met30"], "forbidden_overgeneralizations": ["the only pathway", "all F-box proteins"], "high_risk_check_facts": [{"fact": "The study uses yeast Met30 as a model.", "slot_type": "organism", "source_span": "on the example of yeast Met30 ."}]}, {"article_id": "laysumm_plos_1309", "source_dataset": "PLOS", "original_index": 1309, "mode": "factuality_chase", "evidence_row_id": "row_0005", "abstract_sentence_idx": 6, "abstract_claim": "This ubiquitylation and degradation pathway acts on Met30 that is dissociated from Skp1.", "core_keep_af": "This newly described ubiquitylation and degradation pathway acts on Met30 that is dissociated from Skp1.", "expert_summary_hint": "This pathway targets free F-box proteins and requires part of the SCF core .", "lay_context": "This new pathway specifically targets Met30 proteins that are not bound to Skp1, another key component of the SCF ligase complex. This suggests it deals with 'free' F-box proteins.", "story_role": "mechanism", "support_af_ids": ["laysumm_plos_1309_cand_0006", "laysumm_plos_1309_cand_0034", "laysumm_plos_1309_cand_0073"], "allowed_evidence_spans": ["This ubiquitylation and degradation pathway acts on Met30 that is dissociated from Skp1 .", "These results suggest a proteolytic pathway that recognizes unbound ‘Skp1-free’ Met30 to avoid accumulation of excess unbound Met30 , which could bind substrates and shield them from recognition by fully assembled SCFMet30 complexes .", "Thus , these results provide evidence for existence of an additional , Skp1-independent , degradation pathway for several F-box proteins , which appears to target F-box proteins that are not bound to the SCF core complex ."], "must_use_terms": ["ubiquitylation", "degradation pathway", "Met30", "dissociated from Skp1"], "forbidden_overgeneralizations": ["all Met30", "only Skp1"], "high_risk_check_facts": []}, {"article_id": "laysumm_plos_1309", "source_dataset": "PLOS", "original_index": 1309, "mode": "factuality_ch